WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.977
Hey, I'm Kalvin.

00:00:00.977 --> 00:00:02.930
You've got twenty beds, patients recovering,

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.233
and another admission coming.

00:00:04.233 --> 00:00:05.860
Which bed is actually ready?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.814
Here's the small Jev prototype I

00:00:07.814 --> 00:00:09.442
put together to explore that.

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:10.744
The patients are fictional.

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.698
You can run it, change it,

00:00:12.698 --> 00:00:14.000
and build on it.

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:16.352
The files live on GitHub.

00:00:16.352 --> 00:00:18.724
With Docker Desktop running, clone the

00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:21.097
repository, move into the project folder,

00:00:21.097 --> 00:00:23.469
and run the Compose command shown

00:00:23.469 --> 00:00:25.841
here. Open localhost, port eighty-seven eighty-seven.

00:00:25.841 --> 00:00:27.423
Leave the terminal running.

00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:29.400
The first build needs internet.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.981
Docker packages the app;

00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:33.353
it does not include Jev's model

00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:34.935
or an API key.

00:00:35.292 --> 00:00:37.778
The board shows twenty made-up beds

00:00:37.778 --> 00:00:40.264
across maternity, gynecology, and day surgery.

00:00:40.264 --> 00:00:42.751
There are three available, twelve occupied,

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:45.237
three in turnaround, and two on

00:00:45.237 --> 00:00:47.724
hold. Read each status with its

00:00:47.724 --> 00:00:50.210
estimated time. An unknown estimate stays

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.696
unknown. This board uses a fixed

00:00:52.696 --> 00:00:55.183
demo clock. A countdown never releases

00:00:55.183 --> 00:00:56.012
a bed.

00:00:56.375 --> 00:00:58.511
For a nurse, start by selecting

00:00:58.511 --> 00:01:00.648
a bed. Here we have MAT

00:01:00.648 --> 00:01:02.784
zero two, a fictional cesarean patient,

00:01:02.784 --> 00:01:04.921
pain rated four out of five,

00:01:04.921 --> 00:01:07.057
and four of eight milestones met.

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:09.194
The progress note should say what

00:01:09.194 --> 00:01:11.330
changed and which follow-up is pending.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.467
These examples do not replace a

00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:14.535
care team's assessment.

00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:16.847
Choose the Improving practice note.

00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:19.164
It explicitly says walking and meals

00:01:19.164 --> 00:01:20.709
are better than yesterday.

00:01:20.709 --> 00:01:22.254
Press Show expected answer.

00:01:22.254 --> 00:01:24.571
The result is marked sample only.

00:01:24.571 --> 00:01:26.887
This is a written example answer,

00:01:26.887 --> 00:01:28.432
with no model call.

00:01:28.432 --> 00:01:30.749
It describes improvement in the note,

00:01:30.749 --> 00:01:33.066
but it does not confirm discharge

00:01:33.066 --> 00:01:34.997
or change the bed's status.

00:01:35.375 --> 00:01:36.919
Now choose Needs review.

00:01:36.919 --> 00:01:39.234
Pain is limiting walking, nursing reassessment

00:01:39.234 --> 00:01:41.549
is pending, and departure is delayed.

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:43.093
Those are explicit blockers.

00:01:43.093 --> 00:01:45.408
The written expected answer is needs

00:01:45.408 --> 00:01:47.723
review. That means the observations and

00:01:47.723 --> 00:01:48.881
follow-up need attention.

00:01:48.881 --> 00:01:51.196
It is not an instruction to

00:01:51.196 --> 00:01:53.511
discharge the patient or assign the

00:01:53.511 --> 00:01:55.055
bed to someone else.

00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:57.581
The Unclear case says an update

00:01:57.581 --> 00:01:59.746
was received, but it gives no

00:01:59.746 --> 00:02:01.910
recovery trend. Its written expected category

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:04.075
is unclear. For a live unclear

00:02:04.075 --> 00:02:06.239
answer, the app asks for more

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:08.404
detail about changes in pain, walking

00:02:08.404 --> 00:02:10.208
or meals, and pending reassessment.

00:02:10.208 --> 00:02:12.372
That is feedback on the note,

00:02:12.372 --> 00:02:14.537
not a grade for the nurse.

00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:17.129
If I edit the note, the

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:19.341
old answer clears and the practice

00:02:19.341 --> 00:02:21.553
selection resets. The app says to

00:02:21.553 --> 00:02:23.765
use an unchanged practice case or

00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:24.871
ask Jev again.

00:02:24.871 --> 00:02:27.084
That keeps an answer from quietly

00:02:27.084 --> 00:02:28.558
following a different note.

00:02:28.558 --> 00:02:30.771
Changing the selected bed also clears

00:02:30.771 --> 00:02:31.877
the old result.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.985
Now let's actually ask Jev.

00:02:33.985 --> 00:02:36.068
This server has a key configured,

00:02:36.068 --> 00:02:38.150
so the private field stays empty.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.232
For MAT zero two, I enter

00:02:40.232 --> 00:02:41.274
a fictional update:

00:02:41.274 --> 00:02:43.356
pain limits walking, meals are tolerated,

00:02:43.356 --> 00:02:44.744
and reassessment is pending.

00:02:44.744 --> 00:02:46.132
I click Ask Jev.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.215
The real answer comes back as

00:02:48.215 --> 00:02:50.297
needs review, with a high delay

00:02:50.297 --> 00:02:52.379
score. That's a flag for staff

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:54.462
to review the note and follow-up.

00:02:54.462 --> 00:02:55.850
The bed stays occupied.

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:58.608
Choose Time room cleaning and select

00:02:58.608 --> 00:02:59.808
a turnaround room.

00:02:59.808 --> 00:03:02.208
Start cleaning records the computer's actual

00:03:02.208 --> 00:03:04.608
time. The timer saves room IDs

00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:07.008
and timestamps in this browser, and

00:03:07.008 --> 00:03:08.608
resumes after a reload.

00:03:08.608 --> 00:03:11.008
Finish cleaning records elapsed minutes and

00:03:11.008 --> 00:03:12.608
updates the local average.

00:03:12.608 --> 00:03:15.008
It still says awaiting staff release.

00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:17.408
These few seconds only demonstrate the

00:03:17.408 --> 00:03:19.808
timer; they are not a typical

00:03:19.808 --> 00:03:20.608
cleaning duration.

00:03:20.958 --> 00:03:23.110
There is also a separate readiness

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.261
example you can run from the

00:03:25.261 --> 00:03:27.413
command line. It considers departure windows,

00:03:27.413 --> 00:03:29.564
cleaner availability, breaks, and a shift.

00:03:29.564 --> 00:03:31.715
The two-room example shows estimated windows

00:03:31.715 --> 00:03:33.150
in New York time.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.301
Both rooms remain occupied, with release

00:03:35.301 --> 00:03:37.453
pending. This timing function is not

00:03:37.453 --> 00:03:39.604
connected to the board yet and

00:03:39.604 --> 00:03:41.038
needs no Jev key.

00:03:41.417 --> 00:03:43.577
The separate data pipeline replays saved

00:03:43.577 --> 00:03:45.737
public aggregate counts from New York

00:03:45.737 --> 00:03:47.897
for twenty twenty-three and twenty twenty-four,

00:03:47.897 --> 00:03:50.057
offline. Those stay lengths cover the

00:03:50.057 --> 00:03:52.217
whole admission; they are not individual

00:03:52.217 --> 00:03:54.377
bed-release forecasts. GitHub runs the project

00:03:54.377 --> 00:03:56.537
checks automatically. You can clone it,

00:03:56.537 --> 00:03:58.697
change the examples, and build on

00:03:58.697 --> 00:04:00.857
it. This is a gift for

00:04:00.857 --> 00:04:01.937
learning and review.

00:04:01.937 --> 00:04:03.737
Keep it to fictional patients.
